        <div class="qa-box"><span class="qa-label">Quick answer</span><p><strong>Technical SEO services</strong> fix the foundation that lets search engines <strong>crawl, index and understand</strong> your website: site architecture, indexing, speed and Core Web Vitals, mobile usability, structured data, redirects and security. A good <strong>technical SEO agency</strong> starts with an audit, ranks fixes by impact, and shows results in rankings, traffic and leads. Cost depends on site size, issue count and whether fixes are implemented for you. A 15-point checklist is below.</p></div>
        <p class="updated-note">Last updated November 2026 · Written for US businesses evaluating technical SEO</p>

        <p>You can publish excellent content and still go nowhere in search if Google cannot reliably crawl and understand your site. That is the job of <strong>technical SEO services</strong>: removing the barriers underneath your pages so everything else you invest in can work. This guide explains what a <strong>technical SEO agency</strong> actually does, what drives the cost, how to evaluate a proposal, and gives you a 15-point checklist you can use today.</p>
        <p>For the bigger picture of what an SEO package covers, see our guide to <a href="seo-optimization-services-guide.html">SEO optimization services</a>. This post goes deeper on the technical layer.</p>

        <h2 id="what-is-technical-seo">What are technical SEO services?</h2>
        <p>Search engines work in stages: they discover pages, crawl them, render them, decide whether to index them, and then rank them. Technical SEO makes each stage smooth. If a stage fails, the page may never appear in results, however good its content is. Services in this area usually include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Technical audit:</strong> a full review of crawlability, indexing, speed, mobile usability, structured data and security.</li>
          <li><strong>Prioritized fix list:</strong> issues ranked by impact and effort, so the most valuable work happens first.</li>
          <li><strong>Implementation or developer support:</strong> making the changes, or writing clear tickets for your developers.</li>
          <li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> watching for new errors after site updates, redesigns or migrations.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="what-agency-does">What a technical SEO agency does day to day</h2>
        <p>A <strong>technical SEO agency</strong> works through your site systematically. Typical tasks include analyzing server logs and crawl data to see how Googlebot actually moves through your site, finding pages that are blocked, duplicated or orphaned, improving page speed, fixing redirect chains, adding structured data, and reviewing international or mobile setups. After a redesign or platform migration, a technical specialist is often the difference between keeping rankings and losing them.</p>
        <p>Technical work is especially valuable if you have a large site, an e-commerce store, a recent redesign, a sudden traffic drop, or content that is published but never seems to rank. If your store runs on Shopify, see our guide to <a href="ecommerce-seo-services-shopify.html">e-commerce SEO services for Shopify stores</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="checklist">The 15-point technical SEO checklist</h2>
        <h3>Crawling and indexing</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>XML sitemap.</strong> Submit a clean sitemap in Google Search Console that lists only indexable, canonical URLs.</li>
          <li><strong>Robots.txt.</strong> Confirm you are not accidentally blocking important pages, scripts or styles.</li>
          <li><strong>Index coverage.</strong> Review the Pages report in Search Console for excluded or errored URLs, and find out why.</li>
          <li><strong>Canonical tags.</strong> Make sure each page points to its preferred version, especially where duplicate URLs exist.</li>
          <li><strong>Noindex audit.</strong> Check that no important page carries a stray noindex tag, a common cause of pages vanishing from results.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3>Site structure</h3>
        <ol start="6">
          <li><strong>Clean architecture.</strong> Important pages should be reachable within a few clicks from the homepage.</li>
          <li><strong>Internal linking.</strong> Link related pages together with descriptive anchor text, and fix orphan pages that nothing links to.</li>
          <li><strong>Redirects and errors.</strong> Replace redirect chains with single redirects and fix broken links and 404 pages that still receive links or traffic.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3>Performance and experience</h3>
        <ol start="9">
          <li><strong>Core Web Vitals.</strong> Google treats a Largest Contentful Paint of 2.5 seconds or less, an Interaction to Next Paint of 200 milliseconds or less and a Cumulative Layout Shift of 0.1 or less as good.</li>
          <li><strong>Page speed.</strong> Compress images, use modern formats, defer non-critical scripts and reduce unused code.</li>
          <li><strong>Mobile usability.</strong> Confirm the mobile version has the same content and works properly, since search engines evaluate the mobile experience.</li>
          <li><strong>HTTPS and security.</strong> Serve every page over HTTPS and avoid mixed-content warnings.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3>Understanding and enhancement</h3>
        <ol start="13">
          <li><strong>Structured data.</strong> Add schema markup for your content type, such as Article, FAQ, Product, LocalBusiness and BreadcrumbList, and validate it.</li>
          <li><strong>Duplicate and thin content.</strong> Consolidate or improve near-duplicate and low-value pages.</li>
          <li><strong>Monitoring.</strong> Set up regular checks in Search Console and your analytics so new problems are caught early.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="core-web-vitals">Why Core Web Vitals and speed matter</h2>
        <p>Core Web Vitals measure real user experience: how fast the main content loads, how quickly the page responds to input, and how stable the layout is while loading. Slow or jumpy pages frustrate visitors, which hurts conversions regardless of rankings. Common fixes include optimizing the largest image on the page, reducing JavaScript that blocks interaction, reserving space for images and ads so content does not jump, and using caching and a content delivery network. Test with Google's PageSpeed Insights and the Core Web Vitals report in Search Console.</p>
        <div class="article-pull">"Technical SEO doesn't make a weak page strong. It stops a strong page from being invisible."</div>

        <h2 id="structured-data">Structured data and schema markup</h2>
        <p>Structured data is code that tells search engines what a page is about in a standard format. It can make pages eligible for rich results such as FAQ dropdowns, review stars and product details, and it helps search and AI systems interpret your content and business entity. Use schema types that match what is genuinely on the page, validate it before launch, and never mark up content that visitors cannot see.</p>

        <h2 id="cost">What do technical SEO services cost?</h2>
        <p>Prices vary widely, so be wary of any article quoting a single "average" figure. What matters is what drives the price:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Site size and complexity.</strong> A 20-page brochure site is a very different job from a store with thousands of product pages.</li>
          <li><strong>Number and severity of issues.</strong> A clean site needs light maintenance; a damaged one needs a rebuild of its foundations.</li>
          <li><strong>Who implements the fixes.</strong> An audit with recommendations costs less than an agency making the changes directly.</li>
          <li><strong>Engagement model.</strong> One-time audit, fixed-scope project, or monthly retainer.</li>
          <li><strong>Platform and development access.</strong> Some platforms limit what can be changed, which affects scope.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
          <tr><th>Engagement</th><th>What you get</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
          <tr><td>One-time audit</td><td>A diagnosis and prioritized roadmap</td><td>Teams with developers who can implement</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fixed-scope project</td><td>Specific fixes delivered, such as a migration or speed overhaul</td><td>Redesigns, migrations, one-time problems</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Monthly retainer</td><td>Ongoing monitoring, fixes and reporting</td><td>Large or fast-changing sites</td></tr>
        </table></div>
        <p>When you compare quotes, ask exactly what is included, how many pages are covered, how issues will be prioritized, and how progress will be reported.</p>

        <h2 id="how-to-choose">How to choose a technical SEO agency</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>They explain findings in plain English,</strong> and tie each issue to a business impact.</li>
          <li><strong>They prioritize.</strong> Beware of a 300-item audit with no ranking of what matters.</li>
          <li><strong>They can work with your developers,</strong> or implement directly on your platform.</li>
          <li><strong>They show before-and-after evidence,</strong> such as indexing, speed and traffic changes.</li>
          <li><strong>They never guarantee rankings.</strong> No one controls search results.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Common technical SEO mistakes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Launching a redesign without redirects,</strong> which drops rankings overnight.</li>
          <li><strong>Blocking the site by accident</strong> with a leftover noindex or robots rule from staging.</li>
          <li><strong>Chasing a perfect speed score</strong> instead of fixing the biggest bottleneck.</li>
          <li><strong>Marking up content that is not on the page,</strong> which can trigger penalties.</li>
          <li><strong>Treating it as one-and-done.</strong> Sites change, and new issues appear.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="our-approach">How Tycoon Clap approaches technical SEO</h2>
        <p>Because we build websites and work in analytics as well as marketing, we can find technical issues and fix them directly. We start with an audit tied to your goals, rank issues by impact, implement the high-value fixes, and track results in rankings, traffic and leads. If you would like a straight assessment of your site's technical health, book a strategy call.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>What are technical SEO services?</summary><p class="faq-a">Technical SEO services improve the behind-the-scenes health of a website so search engines can crawl, index, render and understand it. They typically cover site architecture, indexing, site speed and Core Web Vitals, mobile usability, structured data, redirects, security and fixing errors found in an audit.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What does a technical SEO agency do?</summary><p class="faq-a">A technical SEO agency audits your site for issues that hold back rankings, prioritizes fixes by impact, and either implements them or works with your developers. Ongoing work includes monitoring crawl errors, indexing, speed and structured data after site changes.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How much do technical SEO services cost?</summary><p class="faq-a">Cost depends mainly on site size and complexity, the number of issues found, whether the agency implements fixes or only recommends them, and whether the work is a one-time audit, a project or an ongoing retainer. Ask for a scoped proposal and compare deliverables, not just the price.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is the difference between technical SEO and on-page SEO?</summary><p class="faq-a">Technical SEO makes sure search engines can access and understand your site as a whole. On-page SEO optimizes the content and tags of individual pages, such as titles, headings and copy. Both are needed, and technical issues can limit what on-page work achieves.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How long does a technical SEO audit take?</summary><p class="faq-a">A focused audit of a small site can take days, while a large or complex site such as a big e-commerce store can take several weeks. Fixing the issues usually takes longer than finding them, especially if developer time is limited.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Which Core Web Vitals thresholds should I aim for?</summary><p class="faq-a">Google's published guidance treats a Largest Contentful Paint of 2.5 seconds or less, an Interaction to Next Paint of 200 milliseconds or less, and a Cumulative Layout Shift of 0.1 or less as good, measured at the 75th percentile of page loads.</p></details>
        </div><p style="font-size:.88rem;color:var(--ink-dim);line-height:1.7;margin-top:2rem;">Core Web Vitals thresholds reflect Google's published guidance. Confirm current values at web.dev. Tycoon Clap is not affiliated with Google.</p>
